      "license_path": "benchmarks/REQUESTS_LICENSE.txt",
      "prompt": "Add an optional decoded-response byte budget to Requests. Session(max_response_bytes=None) defaults to unlimited; Session.request and its HTTP-method helpers, module-level helpers such as requests.get, and direct Session.send must accept max_response_bytes. An omitted per-request value inherits the session setting; explicit None disables it for that request, including its redirect chain. None or a non-negative non-boolean integer is valid; reject other values with ValueError before the transport is called. Expose the setting as Session.max_response_bytes and preserve it across pickling.\n\nApply the budget to each response independently, including intermediate and final redirects, eager .content/.text reads, streamed iter_content reads and cached responses returned by response hooks. Count actual decoded bytes after content decompression, not Content-Length or Unicode character count. Exact-boundary and empty zero-budget bodies succeed. The live response-wide total must persist across separate iter_content iterators or a partial iterator followed by .content; rereading cached bytes must not add duplicate bytes to that total. Raise before yielding a chunk that makes the cumulative decoded bytes exceed the limit, stop subsequent reads (later read APIs must raise ResponseTooLarge without reading further), and attempt to close the offending response. If close itself fails, preserve ResponseTooLarge and expose the cleanup failure through notes or exception chaining. Expose the same requests.ResponseTooLarge / requests.exceptions.ResponseTooLarge class, deriving from RequestException, with limit, received (the cumulative count at the first crossing), response and request attributes. Eager reads and convenience helpers must surface the same error. Do not leak the custom budget into adapter transport keyword arguments. Existing unlimited requests and encoding/streaming behavior must remain compatible. Add new regression test files without altering existing tests; local HTTP fixtures are allowed.",
